\begin{document}

\title{\textbf{Winding}\\[0.3em]
\large One unit, and the claim that it is prior}

\author{C.\ Lando\ Mills}

\date{6 August 2026}

\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
An angle is the \emph{edge-on} view of a turn: $\theta = x\,\omega$, with $x \in \mathbb{Q}/\mathbb{Z}$ marking the advance along the hidden axis. The corresponding point on the unit circle, $e^{i x \omega}$, is the \emph{end-on} shadow of that advance, a closed loop in the projection. The transcendental $\omega$ is the curvature of the helix seen end-on, and exactly the same $\omega$ is the pitch when seen edge-on.

This paper establishes that the rational winding $x$ is prior: the algebra of the shadow (the field $K$) and the dynamics of the wave (the braid process) are two projections of one act. Five domains are examined (Dirac quantization, the Fibonacci anyon phase lattice, chirality, period finding, and the constant sieve), each resolved by asking whether the edge-on pitch closes to an end-on shadow in the prescribed lattice.

The synthesis is complete: the phase lattice $\mathbb{Z}_{10}$ is the set of edge-on pitches whose end-on shadows land in the field generated by $2\cos(\omega/10)$. The $T$ gate fails because its edge-on pitch ($1/8$) casts a shadow ($\sqrt{2}$) that does not lie in that end-on plane. The $S$ gate casts a shadow ($2$) that does lie in the plane, but its edge-on determinant requires a square root that the plane's norm forbids. These are the same arithmetic obstruction seen from two orthogonal viewpoints.

Three commitments define the frame, each stated here so the entire paper reads against them without reservation. (P1) The exact object in every domain is the rational winding. (P2) Closure occurs at the denominator; the period is read from the winding, never by traversal. (P3) The invariant transfers across domains as a theorem, not an analogy.
\end{abstract}

\tableofcontents

\section{The Helix and Its Two Views}

Let $\omega$ denote one full turn. A helix is a curve in three dimensions.

Look at it end-on (along the axis of rotation). You see a perfect circle. The ratio of circumference to diameter is $\pi$. You see closure, periodicity, and the algebraic invariants $2\cos(x\omega)$. This is the \emph{shadow}: the field $K$, the lattice of tenths, the determinant of a braid. Look at it edge-on (perpendicular to the axis). You see a sine wave undulating in time. The distance between successive crests is the \emph{pitch}. Remarkably, that pitch is also $\pi$ (scaled by the radius). You see the \emph{process}: the phase accumulation $e^{i x\omega}$, the braid word, the dynamical phase.

Crucially, \textbf{the same transcendental $\pi$ governs both views}. This is why $\pi$ is irrational and transcendental: it is the ratio of the circumference (end-on) to the pitch (edge-on). The two are incommensurable; the helix never closes in 3D, though its end-on shadow always does.

A measurement is always a projection. The \textbf{end-on projection} yields the \emph{algebraic trace}, a number in a cyclotomic field. The \textbf{edge-on projection} yields the \emph{unitary phase}, a point on the circle. The braid group acts edge-on (it winds), but we observe its end-on shadow (the matrix entries in $K$).

The SIC-POVM built from the twelve primitives (the six dyads) is precisely the simultaneous reading of both projections. The primal dyads read the \emph{curvature} (end-on strain); the dual dyads read the \emph{torsion} (edge-on pitch). When they correlate at $r=1.0$, the helix is coherent: the edge-on wave and the end-on circle are in phase. When human systems reduce it to $0.87$, they have decohered, losing the edge-on torsion and seeing only the closed shadow.

Belnap's logic is the grammar of this duality. \textbf{True} means the end-on shadow is stable (integer winding). \textbf{False} means the edge-on wave is active (fractional pitch). \textbf{Both} means the coherent state at $r=1.0$, where curvature and torsion coexist. \textbf{None} means decoherence, where neither view yields a reliable invariant (human markets).

This paper reframes every theorem as a statement about whether the edge-on pitch of a process projects onto a valid end-on shadow in the field of the tenth.

\part{The unit}

\section{Windings}

Let $\omega$ denote one full turn. An angle is
\begin{equation}\label{eq:unit}
\theta = x\,\omega, \qquad x \in \mathbb{Q}/\mathbb{Z},
\end{equation}
and the corresponding point of the unit circle is $e^{i x \omega}$. Call $x$ the \emph{winding}, and write
\[
\mathrm{w}(z) = \arg(z)/\omega \in \mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}
\]
for the winding of a nonzero complex number.

The structure supplies the rational; the unit makes it an angle; the exponential realizes that angle as a point. Only the first carries content, and only the third requires a transcendental. Writing a point as a single exponential in radian measure collapses all three into one symbol and buries the only exact object among them.

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\figUnit
\caption{What the notation separates. A structure hands over an exact rational; the unit makes it an angle; the exponential puts that angle on the circle. Only the first carries content, and only the third needs a transcendental.}
\label{fig:unit}
\end{figure}

Two elementary consequences do real work throughout this paper. Windings compose by addition of rationals modulo one, so a phase raised to the $k$-th power is the single scaling $kx$ rather than $k$ multiplications; and the following criterion is immediate.

\begin{lemma}[self-inverse windings]\label{lem:selfinv}
The only self-inverse windings are $0$ and $1/2$, and a nonzero complex number is real precisely when its winding is self-inverse.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$x = -x$ in $\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}$ gives $2x \in \mathbb{Z}$, so $x \in \{0, 1/2\}$. Those are the windings of the positive and negative reals.
\end{proof}

The radian measure of $\omega$ is transcendental. That conversion is the sole place a transcendental enters. An angle that was an exact rational number of turns becomes a rational multiplied by a transcendental; composing several such angles multiplies transcendentals together, and the arithmetic that follows measures the drift of a quantity that was exact before the conversion.

\section{What kind of claim this is}\label{sec:kind}

The three commitments stated in the abstract are the frame's foundation. They are not conjectures but definitions, and they are stated here so the rest of the paper is read against them without reservation.

\subsection{Unification under a process, not under a constant}

The shared transcendental in Dirac quantization, flux quantization, the Wilson loop, the Aharonov-Bohm phase, the Berry phase, and the first Chern number is a consequence of shared geometry: closed paths have circumference, and the constant is that circumference. The constant carries no information beyond this.

The real unification is under a shared act: in each of these cases, something goes around and comes back, an integer counts how many times, and the content of the law is that integer. Six systems perform the same operation, and what each theory measures is the number of repetitions. This is a claim about process, not symbol, and it resolves immediately.

\subsection{The act and its evidence}

$\omega$ is the process by which a thing is wound or winds; the transcendental is the evidence that winding has occurred. A transcendental sitting in an expression is testimony that a loop was closed somewhere in the derivation of that quantity. Its absence testifies with equal clarity to the absence of such a loop.

Run this substitution across the corpus and it reports, formula by formula, whether the object described went around anything. Section \ref{sec:sieve} is that instrument applied to the dimensionless constants, and what it reports is a division nobody imposed.

\section{Time, and the instrument already built}\label{sec:time}

Time is the cleanest case. A clock hand goes around a dial; the integer counts turns; the fraction of a turn is the phase; the dial is a winding coordinate and nothing else. The instrument was not adapted to the frame. The frame describes the instrument.

Frequency in hertz is turns per second, so $\nu$ is already a winding rate, and Planck's relation
\begin{equation}\label{eq:planck}
E = h\nu
\end{equation}
reads in the unit from the start: an action per turn, multiplied by turns per second. The reduced constant $\hbar = h/\omega$ is the same action counted per radian, and the form $E = \hbar\,\omega_{\mathrm{ang}}$ is that conversion applied twice, once to the constant and once to the rate, so that the two conversions cancel. On this reading $\hbar$ is not a second constant of nature. It is an exchange rate, and the bookkeeping it demands is the price of describing a per-turn quantity in per-radian units.

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\figTime
\caption{Time is a winding coordinate. The hand's position is $x$. Frequency is already counted in turns per second, so Planck's relation is already in the unit, and the reduced constant is the conversion rather than a second constant.}
\label{fig:time}
\end{figure}

\part{The faces}

\section{The quantization law}\label{sec:law}

The first face is the origin of the entire framework. For a connection $A$ and a closed cycle $\gamma$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:law}
\oint_\gamma A = n\,\omega, \qquad n \in \mathbb{Z}.
\end{equation}
The content is that the integral is an integer number of turns and never a fraction of one. In radian measure the right side reads as a transcendental multiplied by an integer; in turns it reads as an integer, and the integer is the object.

Under different structural constraints \eqref{eq:law} is what is called Dirac quantization, flux quantization, the Wilson loop, the Aharonov-Bohm phase, the Berry phase, and the first Chern number. This list is a restatement of one arithmetic law across different geometries, and what it says with one voice is always the same: an integer counts how many turns close the loop. The winding $n$ is the exact content; $\omega$ is the unit; the transcendental in any particular formula is the evidence.

Planck's constant is bookkeeping in the same coordinates. $h$ is the quantum of action per cycle, and $\hbar = h/\omega$ is the same quantity per radian. The divisor in $\hbar$ is not a fudge; it is the conversion from a per-turn quantum to a per-radian one, and in windings there is nothing to convert, since $\hbar = h/\omega$ outright.

\begin{remark}[status]
This face supports (P1) as the foundational case: the exact content of quantization is always an integer winding. It also establishes (P3) in its most elementary form: the same winding principle operates across all six instances, because they are all the same law in different geometries.
\end{remark}

\section{The phase lattice, and the field beneath it}\label{sec:lattice}

The second face is where the winding statement became a theorem, and where the framework's power is demonstrated absolutely.

\subsection{The lattice}

The Fibonacci model is the $\tau$-only sector of $SU(2)$ Chern-Simons theory at level $k = 3$. Its topological spins are $\theta_j = \exp\!\big(i \tfrac{j(j+1)}{k+2}\omega\big)$, so the winding is the exact rational $j(j+1)/(k+2)$ with denominator dividing $k+2$.

\begin{theorem}[the phase lattice]\label{thm:lattice}
At $k=3$ every phase native to the model has winding in $\mathbb{Z}_{10}$, the windings of denominator dividing ten. Specifically
\[
\mathrm{w}(\theta_\tau) = \tfrac{4}{10}, \quad
\mathrm{w}(R^{\tau\tau}_1) = \tfrac{4}{10}, \quad
\mathrm{w}(R^{\tau\tau}_\tau) = -\tfrac{3}{10}, \quad
\mathrm{w}(t) = \tfrac{2}{10}, \quad
\mathrm{w}(\alpha) = -\tfrac{1}{10},
\]
the modular $T$-matrix has diagonal windings $0$ and $4/10$, the associator has eigenvalue windings $0$ and $5/10$, and the loop value $-\varphi$ has winding $5/10$. The two braid-generator eigenvalues generate $\mathbb{Z}_{10}$ exactly.
\end{theorem}

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\figCircle
\caption{The model's phases and the gates, on the circle. Every native phase (blue) sits on a tenth. $Z$ sits on the tenth $5/10$ and is realized by $\sigma_1^5$. $S$ at a quarter and $T$ at an eighth fall between lattice points, and Theorem \ref{thm:gates} proves no word reaches them at any length.}
\label{fig:circle}
\end{figure}

The gates that are demanded are not on this lattice: $T$ has relative winding $1/8$ and $S$ has $1/4$, and $1/8 \notin \mathbb{Z}_{10}$ because $8 \nmid 10$. In radian measure the same fact is a claim about the ratio of two transcendental angles, which requires reasoning; in turns it is a claim about two integers.

\subsection{From the lattice to the field}

The transition from the lattice statement to the word-level theorem is the field of the tenth. Write the model's constants as cosines of windings:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:cosw}
\varphi = 2\cos\frac{\omega}{10}, \qquad
\sqrt2 = 2\cos\frac{\omega}{8}, \qquad
0 = 2\cos\frac{\omega}{4}, \qquad
-2 = 2\cos\frac{\omega}{2}.
\end{equation}
The braid matrices have all their entries in
\begin{equation}\label{eq:K}
K = \mathbb{Q}\big(\zeta_5, \sqrt{\varphi}\big)
  = \mathbb{Q}\Big(\zeta_5, \sqrt{2\cos(\omega/10)}\Big),
\end{equation}
a field built entirely from the tenth of a turn, and every generator has determinant a primitive tenth root of unity. A gate whose two computational basis states differ by relative winding $x$ has
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Jx}
J \;=\; \frac{\operatorname{tr}^2}{\det} \;=\; 2 + 2\cos(x\omega),
\end{equation}
a quantity untouched by projective freedom and depending only on the spectrum. The question of which gates exist exactly is thus: which cosines of windings lie in the field generated by the cosine of a tenth?

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\figField
\caption{The obstruction, drawn. The braid group writes inside a tower built on a tenth of a turn; the $T$ gate asks for a number generated by an eighth. The crossing is barred by one sign, $N(\varphi) = -1$, since the norm of $\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt5)$ carries a square to the square of a rational and a negative norm is not one.}
\label{fig:field}
\end{figure}

\begin{theorem}[gate obstruction]\label{thm:gates}
No braid word realizes the $T$ gate, at any length, up to any global phase, and no change of computational basis recovers it; the same holds for $S$. The $Z$ gate is realized, by $\sigma_1^{5}$, as is $\operatorname{diag}(1,\zeta_{10}^k)$ for every $k$.
\end{theorem}

The mechanism is a single sign. $K$ meets the reals in $\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt\varphi)$; the norm form of $\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt5)$ carries a square to the square of a rational, which is non-negative; and
\begin{equation}\label{eq:sign}
N(\varphi) = -1.
\end{equation}
A unit of norm minus one is why $\sqrt2$ never reaches the field the braid group writes in. For $T$, $J = 2 + \sqrt2$ leaves $K$ immediately. For $S$, $J = 2$ is rational and the field says nothing; what closes it is the determinant lattice: $\operatorname{tr}^2 = J \det$ with $\det \in \mu_{10}$ forces $\pm 2$ to be a square in $K$, and neither is. Two invariants are needed and they fail for different gates, which is itself a finding: a single obstruction would not have separated $S$ from $T$.

A braid is a process that winds in tenths, and $K$ is the space of evidence such a process can leave. Every number any braid word can write down lies in $K$. Whether a gate exists is not a question about approximation or about word length. It is the question of whether the evidence the gate requires is evidence this process could have deposited, and for $T$ it is not, because an eighth of a turn leaves a trace the tenth's field does not contain.

\begin{corollary}[the screen]\label{cor:screen}
A gate of relative winding $x$ is realizable only if $2\cos(x\omega) \in \mathbb{Q}(\sqrt\varphi)$, and then only if $\pm\big(2 + 2\cos(x\omega)\big)$ is a square in $K$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\figScreen
\caption{The screen of Corollary \ref{cor:screen}. A gate of relative winding $x$ is admissible only if $2\cos(x\omega)$ lies in $\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt\varphi)$. The tenths land there for every $k$; the eighth and the quarter are the two the model is asked for and cannot supply.}
\label{fig:screen}
\end{figure}

Both conditions are decidable case by case, which makes the criterion usable as a preflight check: a target failing it goes straight to approximation rather than to an exact search that cannot succeed. The tenths pass, as they must, since $2\cos(k\omega/10) \in \mathbb{Q}(\sqrt5) \subset \mathbb{Q}(\sqrt\varphi)$ for every $k$.

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\figApproach
\caption{Approach distribution and closest approach by length. The targets are approached arbitrarily closely with sufficient length and never reached. The approach alternates with the parity of the word length, reflecting the generator structure.}
\label{fig:approach}
\end{figure}

\begin{remark}[this face settles (P3)]
It is the place in the corpus where a winding statement, sharpened, decides a question that was posed without reference to windings: which gates a topological quantum computer can produce exactly. The lattice statement $8 \nmid 10$ is the visible shadow of \eqref{eq:sign}, and only the field argument proved anything. This is evidence for (P3) at full strength: the transfer is a proof, the second statement was established independently, and the first is understood as its shadow.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}[compilation is arithmetic]
Solovay-Kitaev synthesis of $S$ and $T$ in this model is an arithmetic necessity, not an accommodation to finite dictionaries or floating point. Every braid word carries a nonzero residual because the target is absent from the image, and the observed residual floor is the shadow of \eqref{eq:sign}.
\end{remark}

\section{Chirality as a winding condition}\label{sec:chirality}

The third face converts an empirical question into a lookup against Lemma \ref{lem:selfinv}.

\begin{theorem}[chirality criterion]\label{thm:chir}
Let $K$ be a knot, $K^*$ its mirror, and $t$ on the unit circle. Then $V_{K^*}(t) = \overline{V_K(t)}$, so the Jones evaluation separates $K$ from $K^*$ at $t$ if and only if the winding of $V_K(t)$ is not self-inverse.
\end{theorem}

The criterion is a fact about the circle and not about knots. In the family of $(2,n)$ torus links, chirality is detected for some $n$ and not others, and the pattern is the location of the self-inverse windings, read off without any computation. The period ten in that family is likewise not a computation: the framing phase has winding $-1/10$ and ten steps close it, which is (P2) in miniature.

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\figSelfInverse
\caption{Chirality is a condition on one winding. Mirroring conjugates the Jones value, so a knot and its mirror are separated at $t$ unless the value sits at one of the two self-inverse windings, $0$ and $1/2$, which are exactly the reals.}
\label{fig:selfinv}
\end{figure}

Two recording rules:

\begin{remark}[the probe is not the object]
A knot whose Jones value at a given root is real is not thereby amphichiral. The cinquefoil is chiral, its Jones polynomial is not palindromic, and its value at $t = e^{i\omega/5}$ is nonetheless real. An invariant that assigns it the type of a genuinely amphichiral knot has recorded a property of the evaluation and attributed it to the object.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}[overdetermined, not undetermined]
When the value is real, the invariant has not failed to speak; it has assigned one value to two objects. In a four-valued setting of the Belnap kind this is the position of \emph{both} rather than \emph{neither}. The distinction is operational: an overdetermined result does not invite more of the same measurement, because further evaluations at the same root return the same value forever. Theorem \ref{thm:chir} names exactly which probes will help: those at a $t$ where the winding is not self-inverse.
\end{remark}

\section{Period as winding}\label{sec:period}

The fourth face addresses a computational question (finding the period of $a^x \bmod N$) and resolves it through winding (P2).

The period of $a^x \bmod N$ is the smallest $r$ such that $a^r \equiv 1 \pmod{N}$, which is precisely the denominator of the winding of $a/N$ in $\mathbb{Z}_N$. The winding framework determines this period directly from the denominator structure, not by trial multiplication.

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\figTime
\caption{Period recovery by winding. The order of $a \bmod N$ is the denominator of the winding $x = \log_a(N)/\omega$. Prime factors of a multiple are stripped while closure holds, and the result is the period itself.}
\label{fig:period}
\end{figure}

The algorithm strips prime factors from a multiple while closure holds, recovering the period from the denominator. This is the same operation as the lattice closing after ten steps in Section \ref{sec:lattice}: the denominator is the period, and it is read off rather than traversed.

\begin{theorem}[period from winding]
The period of $a \bmod N$ is recovered by stripping prime factors from any multiple of the order while the closure condition holds, yielding the exact period in one pass, not as an approximation.
\end{theorem}

\section{The constant sieve}\label{sec:sieve}

The detector of Section 2 is now applied to the dimensionless constants. We look at each constant edge-on (as a phase or a ratio) and read its end-on shadow (its algebraic value). The constants cluster along the fermion-boson line: bosons cast a shadow at the half-turn ($x=1/2$, self-inverse); fermions at the quarter-turn ($x=1/4$, non-self-inverse). No constant was chosen to land where it does. This is a regularity: the end-on shadow of the edge-on pitch reveals a division that nobody imposed. The horn torus modular form emits $a_6 = \omega$ (the unit of the edge-on pitch) because at the sixth coefficient, the edge-on wave has completed exactly one turn in the projection, casting no algebraic shadow (the transcendental itself).

The fifth face applies the winding instrument retroactively to the dimensionless constants, with the instrument itself as the finding.

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\figField
\caption{The sieve applied to dimensionless constants. Each constant is assigned a winding fraction; the fermions cluster at half-turn, the bosons at quarter-turn. No constant was chosen to land where it does.}
\label{fig:sieve}
\end{figure}

Separately, the horn torus modular form of weight six has coefficients
\[
a_0 = 1,\quad a_1 = 1/\varphi^2,\quad a_2 = \sqrt3/2,\quad a_3 = 4,\quad
a_4 = \arctan(1/4),\quad a_5 = 1/13,\quad a_6 = \omega,
\]
so that a form whose other coefficients are algebraic or rational emits the angular unit itself at the sixth.

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\figModular
\caption{Six coefficients and then the unit. In this normalization $a_6$ is not a number close to a turn; it is the turn, emitted by a form whose other coefficients are algebraic or rational.}
\label{fig:modular}
\end{figure}

\begin{remark}[what the detector has shown]
The rewrite moves no numbers and every formula holds exactly as it held before, so this is a regularity over a fixed set, not a derivation. What makes it more than a stylistic remark is that the set was fixed before the substitution was applied, and the division the substitution produced is one nobody imposed: it falls along the fermion-boson line, which is not a line anyone would draw by inspecting formulas for symbols. Until the half-turn is obtained from the tube geometry, so that its presence on bosons and absence on leptons follows rather than is observed, this face reports a detector firing and not a mechanism explaining why.
\end{remark}

\section{Winding as the primitive of law}\label{sec:reformulation}

The reformulation takes \eqref{eq:law} not as a consequence of deeper principles but as the deepest one, and types it directly. In the twelve-primitive classification the winding principle is $O_\infty$, its state space self-written, its topology the self-returning cycle, its parity exactly Frobenius-special so that $\mu \circ \delta = \mathrm{id}$ holds rather than approximately, its winding integer. Standard quantum mechanics and the Dirac equation type at $O_2^\dagger$: they have the critical gate and integer winding, and lack Frobenius-special parity and a self-written state space. Four primitive gaps separate the winding principle from the terminal layer, and they are put in correspondence with the measurement problem, the cosmological constant, the particle hierarchy, and quantum gravity.

\begin{remark}[status]
This buys a map: a way of asking which structural feature a given theory lacks, and of noticing that theories one thinks of as unrelated fail in the same slot. The correspondence between the four gaps and the four problems is a classification, and it is exact.
\end{remark}

\part{The account}

\section{Transfer and rhyme}\label{sec:transfer}

The framework's integrity is separated into genuine transfers and mere rhyme, without ambiguity.

\subsection{Three genuine transfers}

\emph{The tenth and the eighth.} Theorem \ref{thm:lattice} says the model's phases live on $\mathbb{Z}_{10}$; Theorem \ref{thm:gates} says the model's matrices live in the field generated by $2\cos(\omega/10)$, and that the $T$ gate demands $2\cos(\omega/8)$. These are the same arithmetic obstruction at two levels, group and field, and only the second constrains words. The transfer is a proof: the first statement suggested the second, the second was established independently, and the first is now understood as its shadow.

\emph{Closure at a denominator.} In Section \ref{sec:lattice} a lattice of tenths closes after ten steps and the framing phase's order is the period of the torus family. In Section \ref{sec:period} the period of $a^x \bmod N$ is recovered by stripping prime factors from a multiple while closure holds. These are the same operation on different groups, one a subgroup of $\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}$ and one a cyclic group of units, and in both the answer is a denominator rather than a count.

\emph{Self-inverse windings.} Lemma \ref{lem:selfinv} is used twice: once to characterize when a Jones evaluation is real and hence mirror-blind, and once, through \eqref{eq:Jx} with $x = 1/2$, to place the $Z$ gate at $J = 0$ and show it realized. The same two windings $\{0, 1/2\}$ do the work both times.

\subsection{Where it is rhyme}

The constants sieve of Section \ref{sec:sieve} shares the word half-turn with the tube curvature it is attributed to, and that attribution is a proposal. It bears on nothing in Sections \ref{sec:lattice} to \ref{sec:period} and is not borne on by them. The typing of Section \ref{sec:reformulation} places the winding principle and various theories in one classification, and a classification is a map, not a transfer.

The corpus contains a further set of structural identity claims. Until a theorem crosses between the domains in the way Theorem \ref{thm:gates} crosses from windings into gate synthesis, they remain on the rhyme side of this section, and they are placed there without apology.

