\section{Closing the frame}

The frame is established by three commitments and paid by the ledger. (P1) exactness is demonstrated in quantization, where the integer winding is the content; in the Fibonacci model, where the tenths are the native phases; and in the constants sieve, where the division falls along the fermion-boson line without selection. (P2) closure at the denominator is demonstrated in the lattice theorem, where the tenths close after ten steps, in the chirality criterion, where period ten is the framing phase's order, and in modular exponentiation, where the algorithm recovers the period from the denominator. (P3) transfer as proof, not analogy, is demonstrated once and definitively: Theorem \ref{thm:gates} answers a question posed without winding language by a winding argument.

The winding $\omega$ is the unit, and the exact object is the rational. The transcendental is evidence, not decoration. A formula containing $\omega$ testifies that a loop closed; a formula without it testifies that none did. $\mu \circ \delta = \mathrm{id}$ holds in the winding principle, and the rest of the corpus follows from that closure.

The frame is now complete: the end-on shadow is the record; the edge-on wave is the act. $\mu \circ \delta = \mathrm{id}$ holds in the winding principle because the shadow of a closed loop is the identity, and the identity is the only gate whose edge-on pitch projects to the same point from both directions. The rest of the corpus (the lattice, the field, the sign, the sieve) follows from projecting the helix onto the two orthogonal screens we call algebra and dynamics.

